    "body": "The U.S. Postal Service reported a $6.5 billion net loss for fiscal 2026, down from $9 billion a year earlier. Operating revenue rose 3% to $82.9 billion in the year ended Sept. 30, helped by growth in its Ground Advantage package service and higher postage rates.\nWhat’s Related\nHigher revenue and a $2.4 billion drop in workers’ compensation expense helped narrow the loss. USPS attributed much of that expense change to a reassessment of existing claims and changes in the rates used to estimate future costs. Its controllable loss, which excludes certain expenses outside management’s control, fell to $2.4 billion from $2.7 billion.\nThe Postal Service cut 19 million work hours and made changes to how it moves mail and packages by air and ground. Transportation expenses still rose by $225 million, driven in part by higher fuel costs. Compensation and benefits expenses rose by $456 million.\nPackage revenue rises as volume falls\nShipping and package revenue rose $1.7 billion, or 5.1%, to $34.1 billion. USPS handled 359 million fewer packages than the year before, a 5.3% drop. A temporary price increase that took effect in April helped lift revenue.\nGround Advantage was a bright spot. Revenue from the service rose 17.1% to about $19.6 billion, and volume grew 10.5% to nearly 3.3 billion packages. Ground Advantage offers delivery in two to five days for packages weighing up to 70 pounds.\nFirst-Class Mail and Marketing Mail also brought in more revenue even as both handled fewer pieces. For parcel shippers , a separate round of holiday price increases took effect Oct. 4 and is scheduled to end Jan. 17.\nUSPS asks Congress to raise borrowing limit\nThe Postal Service is asking Congress to raise its $15 billion borrowing limit, which has been in place since 1992. It is also seeking changes to how pension costs are divided between USPS and the Treasury, along with more flexibility to invest pension and retiree health funds.\n“Because of our ongoing financial crisis, we are continuing to balance the needs of our daily business operations with our need to conserve cash,” Postmaster General David Steiner said. USPS deferred about $2.6 billion in federal retirement contributions during the fiscal year, payments it will eventually have to make.\nEarlier this year, USPS warned it could run out of cash as soon as 2027. In June, Steiner told lawmakers that revised projections put a potential cash shortfall between 2031 and 2034. The agency continues to ask Congress to change or help pay for some of the requirements that add to its costs.\nVisit Logistics Management to read Jeff Berman’s full story.",
